From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 26 Nov 2026 06:42:22 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] shaders/lut: tetrahedral interpolation as the first vertex and
 the steps along the edges

sh_lut() interpolates a 3D table (pl_shader_custom_lut(), the ICC
profiles) in the tetrahedron of the cube that holds the point, as a sum
of the four vertices weighted by (1 - s.x), (s.x - s.y), (s.y - s.z) and
s.z. Each weight and each product is rounded: an identity table gives the
input back but for the last bit (one float in a hundred of a picture, up
to 2^-24 apart), and a code of the picture rounded at a half then comes
out one code apart.

The same sum is taken as the first vertex plus each weight times the
step along an edge of the tetrahedron: c0 + s.x (c1 - c0) + s.y (c2 -
c1) + s.z (c3 - c2). It is the same interpolation; the rounding now
follows the steps. For an identity, the steps across an axis are 0 and
the one along it is exact where the grid has a power of two plus one
points (17, 33, 65: the coordinate and the table both exact in floats),
so the input comes back to the bit.
---
diff --git a/src/shaders/lut.c b/src/shaders/lut.c
index b443a7a..23c1a3d 100644
--- a/src/shaders/lut.c
+++ b/src/shaders/lut.c
@@ -800,11 +800,19 @@ next_dim: ; // `continue` out of the inner loop
         }
 
         // Interpolate in barycentric coordinates, with four texel fetches
-        GLSLH("    return (1.0 - s.x) * "$"(v0) +   \n"
-              "           (s.x - s.y) * "$"(v1) +   \n"
-              "           (s.y - s.z) * "$"(v2) +   \n"
-              "           (s.z)       * "$"(v3);    \n"
-              "}                                    \n",
+        //
+        // Hades: the same weights summed as the first vertex and the steps
+        // along the edges of the tetrahedron. Summed as weights, (1.0 - s.x)
+        // and the four products are each rounded: an identity table gave the
+        // input but for the last bit. As steps, those of an identity across
+        // an axis are 0 and the one along it is exact where the grid has a
+        // power of two plus one points (17, 33, 65: the coordinate and the
+        // table both exact in floats), so it gives back the input to the bit.
+        GLSLH("    %s c0 = "$"(v0), c1 = "$"(v1), c2 = "$"(v2), c3 = "$"(v3); \n"
+              "    return c0 + s.x * (c1 - c0) + s.y * (c2 - c1) +              \n"
+              "           s.z * (c3 - c2);                                      \n"
+              "}                                                                \n",
+              vartypes[PL_VAR_FLOAT][params->comps - 1],
               int_lut, int_lut, int_lut, int_lut);
     }
 
